{
  "_about": [
    "Journeys page data (site/journeys.qmd). Edit this file, then run: python3 scripts/build_journeys.py (writes _journeys.qmd) and re-render.",
    "Source of every fact: Henry's own account (2026-11-05) and his route images. Public text is English, one line per entry.",
    "home: the place id of Henry's hometown (its province's tile says 'home' instead of a first-visit year).",
    "chapters: one per place lived, in time order; colour = [day, night] (validated as a categorical set for adjacent chapters).",
    "places: id -> name, admin (ISO 3166-2 style: CN-xx for China's provincial-level divisions, US-xx for U.S. states and DC), country (ISO alpha-2; HK for Hong Kong as a region), lat/lon (approximate city centres), lived (the chapter whose colour a place lived in takes when it has no chapter of its own).",
    "entries: date (YYYY-MM, or YYYY-MM-DD where the day is shown), optional end and when (the date words shown), chapter, text, places (the stops this entry claims: dots on the map), optional parent (a stop of a longer journey), transit (place ids landed at between flights) and noentry (transits without entering the country), first (list of 'first' badges), milestone (badge words), route or routes.",
    "route: mode ground (by road or rail), flight, or unknown (way of travel not recorded); path = place ids and '@lat,lon' shaping points (shaping points make no claim of a visit; every mapped route is approximate); passes = provincial divisions or states passed through (they light the tiles; a code ending in '??' is pending and not lit); km = a distance Henry gave (otherwise the length of the drawn line is used).",
    "summit (on a place): peak, m (elevation in metres, from the public source in src), tag; by (on a route or an entry): plane, train, bus, car, bike or foot (the transport icons); by_words: how a card words a route's transport when it is mixed; by_draft: a note when the transport is assumed; title (on an entry): its short name in the almanac; regions (on an entry): provinces or states visited where no place is named yet (they light the tiles and count, without a dot); days (on an entry): exact days kept in the data, not shown; hike (on an entry): place, dist (as Henry gave it, no added precision) and tag, shown as a hiking badge.",
    "list: false keeps an entry off the chapter list (it still draws its dots and routes, counts, and shows in the map cards): the list records the journeys selectively (Henry, v4).",
    "stamp (on an entry): the short name on its passport stamp (hub: true marks a stamp for a transit hub); an entry with firsts gets one stamp per first, a milestone gets a degree stamp.",
    "draft: a short note on what was inferred; on an entry, a place, or a pass (a code ending in '?'). Henry confirms or corrects these; the build lists them."
  ],
  "updated": "2026-11",
  "since": 2010,
  "home": "shouguang",
  "sources": "Map data, loaded by the page: China, with its provinces, Taiwan, Hong Kong, Macao and the South China Sea inset, from the China standard map as published with <a href=\"https://github.com/apache/echarts/tree/4.9.0/map\">Apache ECharts 4.9.0</a> (Apache-2.0); other countries from <a href=\"https://www.naturalearthdata.com/\">Natural Earth</a> (public domain) through <a href=\"https://github.com/topojson/world-atlas\">world-atlas</a> 2.0.2; U.S. states from the U.S. Census Bureau through <a href=\"https://github.com/topojson/us-atlas\">us-atlas</a> 3.0.1. Summit elevations: Mount Tai (1,532.7 m) and Mount Song (Junji Peak, 1,491.7 m) from the 2007 national survey of famous mountains by the State Bureau of Surveying and Mapping (as reported by People's Daily and cited by <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Tai\">Wikipedia</a>); the others from Wikipedia. Routes are drawn through approximate waypoints, not along the roads.",
  "chapters": [
    {"id": "baotou", "name": "Baotou", "where": "Inner Mongolia, China", "from": "2010-09", "to": "2014-07", "line": "A B.A. in English at Inner Mongolia University of Science and Technology, farther from home than I had ever been. Four years on the northern edge of the country, and the first journeys of my own.", "color": ["#d97706", "#c08316"]},
    {"id": "shanghai-bali", "name": "Shanghai and Bali", "where": "China and Indonesia", "from": "2014-07", "to": "2016-09", "line": "A first job in Shanghai, thirteen months on Bali at a power plant under construction, and an island that taught me to dive, to fly and to surf; then back in Shanghai, preparing for the next posting abroad.", "color": ["#db2777", "#d4569a"]},
    {"id": "lagos", "name": "Lagos", "where": "Nigeria", "from": "2016-09", "to": "2019-04", "line": "Two and a half years based in Lagos, working in West Africa; the way home ran through the Gulf and Istanbul.", "color": ["#ea580c", "#dc6a2c"], "note": "Flight pairings and months are approximate: one arc per hub route (Henry, 2026-11-05)."},
    {"id": "san-marcos", "name": "San Marcos", "where": "Texas, United States", "from": "2019-08", "to": "2021-07", "line": "The first American home, a university town in central Texas, and a second master's at Texas State University, ending with six months in Washington, DC.", "color": ["#0d9488", "#139484"]},
    {"id": "dallas", "name": "Dallas", "where": "Texas, United States", "from": "2021-07", "to": "2024-08", "line": "The Ph.D. years at The University of Texas at Dallas, when the map of the United States began to fill in: long drives, first in company and then alone.", "color": ["#2563eb", "#3f83e8"]},
    {"id": "boston", "name": "Boston", "where": "Massachusetts, United States", "from": "2024-08", "to": "2025-01", "line": "A half-year internship in Boston, reached by car across the South, and autumn in New England from Walden Pond to the coast of Maine.", "color": ["#e11d48", "#e2475f"]},
    {"id": "idaho-falls", "name": "Idaho Falls", "where": "Idaho, United States", "from": "2025-01", "to": "2025-12", "line": "An internship at Idaho National Laboratory, reached by car across the whole country; Yellowstone nearby, the Ph.D. defended, the long drive south, and commencement in Dallas.", "color": ["#0891b2", "#148fae"]},
    {"id": "manhattan", "name": "Manhattan", "where": "Kansas, United States", "from": "2026-04", "to": null, "line": "In spring 2026 an offer from Kansas State University, and a postdoc at the edge of the Flint Hills. The log stays open.", "color": ["#4f46e5", "#6d74ea"]}
